WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:02.735
-Our next guest
is a Tony-nominated actor

00:00:02.802 --> 00:00:04.938
you know from films
such as "Pride &amp; Prejudice"

00:00:05.004 --> 00:00:06.905
and the "Pirates
of the Caribbean" franchise,

00:00:06.972 --> 00:00:09.208
as well as shows like "The Night
Manager" and "The White Lotus."

00:00:09.275 --> 00:00:13.612
He stars in the limited series
"Feud: Capote Vs. The Swans,"

00:00:13.679 --> 00:00:17.016
which premieres with two
episodes on January 31st on FX

00:00:17.083 --> 00:00:18.384
and streams
the next day on Hulu.

00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:19.752
Let's take a look.

00:00:19.818 --> 00:00:24.289
-I know about you.
I know about your car accident.

00:00:24.356 --> 00:00:27.960
Shattered your cheekbones.

00:00:28.027 --> 00:00:29.595
How when they rebuilt your face,

00:00:29.662 --> 00:00:31.764
you were suddenly
more beautiful than ever.

00:00:31.830 --> 00:00:37.070
You became a swan from
an ugly duckling, am I right?

00:00:37.136 --> 00:00:40.039
-How do you know
about all that?

00:00:40.106 --> 00:00:45.344
-Well, my point is,
the accident was a blessing.

00:00:45.411 --> 00:00:47.647
And now there's
something else.

00:00:50.283 --> 00:00:52.185
You have a new best friend.

00:00:52.252 --> 00:00:54.620
-[ Laughs ]

00:00:54.687 --> 00:00:57.490
-Now let's have a cigarette.

00:00:57.557 --> 00:01:00.192
-Please welcome to the show
Tom Hollander, everyone.

00:01:00.259 --> 00:01:02.194
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:02.261 --> 00:01:10.470 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:11.970 --> 00:01:15.574
-It's so -- Can I tell you
something I love, Tom?

00:01:15.641 --> 00:01:18.578
I love when the actor
is unrecognizable from the clip.

00:01:18.644 --> 00:01:20.280
That's very exciting to me.

00:01:20.346 --> 00:01:22.248
It's a real transformation.

00:01:22.315 --> 00:01:24.517
-Yeah. No. Yeah, yeah.
It was.

00:01:24.584 --> 00:01:29.222
There were a lot of people
involved in that transformation.

00:01:29.288 --> 00:01:31.090
A lot of restaurants.

00:01:31.157 --> 00:01:33.025
-A lot of restaurants. Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:01:33.092 --> 00:01:35.127
-It was -- I mean,
I would imagine this is --

00:01:35.194 --> 00:01:39.131
this is based on a book
about later-era Truman Capote

00:01:39.198 --> 00:01:41.367
and his friendship with
socialites in New York City...

00:01:41.434 --> 00:01:43.302
-That's right.
-...that he called his swans.

00:01:43.369 --> 00:01:47.006
And this is a sort of darker
period of Truman Capote's life.

00:01:47.073 --> 00:01:50.443
Did you know about
this era of Truman Capote?

00:01:50.510 --> 00:01:54.113
-I knew that he was
a terrible drunk

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:58.117
and that Studio 54 and --

00:01:58.184 --> 00:02:01.654
but I also knew him as a writer.

00:02:01.721 --> 00:02:04.824
I had read his stuff
when I was young.

00:02:04.890 --> 00:02:06.960
I didn't know how dark it went.

00:02:07.026 --> 00:02:11.063
And I didn't know about
his close relationship

00:02:11.130 --> 00:02:13.399
with these fancy ladies

00:02:13.466 --> 00:02:17.837
and how they -- how he
destroyed himself with them.

00:02:17.904 --> 00:02:21.040
He was kind of addicted
to fancy people

00:02:21.107 --> 00:02:23.542
in the same way
that he was addicted to booze.

00:02:23.609 --> 00:02:28.213
He felt these things
could make him whole and --

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:31.617
but he also kind of despised it
and despised himself.

00:02:31.684 --> 00:02:33.852
So he kind of blew it up
and ended up --

00:02:33.919 --> 00:02:36.289
It's a dark tale.
Yeah.

00:02:36.355 --> 00:02:37.557
-No, I should say
it's a dark tale,

00:02:37.624 --> 00:02:41.060
but it is still --
it is a fun char--

00:02:41.127 --> 00:02:42.695
I mean, "character"
is the wrong word,

00:02:42.762 --> 00:02:44.264
but he's a fun person
to play, right?

00:02:44.330 --> 00:02:46.999
I mean, it just might seem
like the juiciest role.

00:02:47.066 --> 00:02:49.802
Yeah, he's one of these people
that leaves --

00:02:49.869 --> 00:02:53.071
when they die,
they leave endless opportunities

00:02:53.138 --> 00:02:55.375
for actors to come along.

00:02:55.441 --> 00:02:58.511
I'm giving my Truman Capote now.

00:02:58.578 --> 00:03:00.647
Yeah, he was
a larger-than-life character,

00:03:00.713 --> 00:03:03.649
and he's an icon
of American literature

00:03:03.716 --> 00:03:07.720
and society.

00:03:07.787 --> 00:03:12.825
And so I was very, very glad

00:03:12.891 --> 00:03:15.561
and lucky
to be offered the part,

00:03:15.628 --> 00:03:17.463
and I loved every second of it.

00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:20.032
-He obviously has
a very distinctive voice,

00:03:20.099 --> 00:03:21.333
speaking style.

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:23.502
How do you approach it
where it's not --

00:03:23.569 --> 00:03:25.538
you're embodying the man

00:03:25.605 --> 00:03:29.409
without doing what seems like
or looks like an impression?

00:03:29.475 --> 00:03:32.278
-Well, you have to do
a little bit of an impression.

00:03:32.345 --> 00:03:35.181
And then you have to
allow yourself to go,

00:03:35.248 --> 00:03:38.383
"Well, I'm not exactly
like Truman Capote.

00:03:38.450 --> 00:03:39.986
I don't look like
Truman Capote,"

00:03:40.052 --> 00:03:43.990
as you can see from the clip,
but you're giving a performance

00:03:44.056 --> 00:03:45.891
where anyone watching it
would go,

00:03:45.958 --> 00:03:49.061
"Well, that can only be
Truman Capote."

00:03:49.128 --> 00:03:50.796
It's not,
"That's Truman Capote."

00:03:50.863 --> 00:03:54.033
It's, "That's Truman Capote."

00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:57.903
So I was doing that,
and Robbie Baitz,

00:03:57.970 --> 00:04:00.306
who's the genius writer
who wrote it, said,

00:04:00.372 --> 00:04:02.141
"You don't have to be
Truman Capote.

00:04:02.208 --> 00:04:04.277
We're playing
Truman Capote the myth."

00:04:04.344 --> 00:04:06.246
And it's eight-episode
extravaganza,

00:04:06.312 --> 00:04:08.648
which is a sort of fantasia,
some of it.

00:04:08.715 --> 00:04:10.716
It keeps going 'round
and 'round in time.

00:04:10.783 --> 00:04:14.120
You see the same events
presented in different ways.

00:04:14.187 --> 00:04:18.524
Each episode is wildly different
tonally from the next episode.

00:04:18.591 --> 00:04:22.762
They really threw
the kitchen sink at it.

00:04:22.829 --> 00:04:24.096
Is that the expression?
-Yeah.

00:04:24.163 --> 00:04:25.564
-Yeah.
-Nailed it.

00:04:25.631 --> 00:04:27.733
-Some kitchen sinks

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:30.369
and some more glamorous things
than kitchen sinks.

00:04:30.435 --> 00:04:33.072
Yeah, it was amazing.

00:04:33.138 --> 00:04:34.907
-I know you've worked
in New York on Broadway.

00:04:34.973 --> 00:04:36.875
You've been here before, but you
got to shoot this in New York

00:04:36.942 --> 00:04:40.879
and also obviously on sets that
were New York from a bygone era.

00:04:40.946 --> 00:04:43.982
Did you like those styles,
that fashion, the look of it?

00:04:44.049 --> 00:04:46.552
-Yes, the whole thing
was like a sort of --

00:04:46.619 --> 00:04:49.154
'Cause I feel, you know, I'm a
tourist when I come to America.

00:04:49.221 --> 00:04:51.090
And this was like
historical tourism.

00:04:51.157 --> 00:04:54.626
I was seeing this world
of the Upper East Side

00:04:54.693 --> 00:04:57.196
and these fancy houses
and the Hamptons

00:04:57.263 --> 00:04:58.530
and the way they lived.

00:04:58.597 --> 00:05:00.099
And the sets are astonishing.

00:05:00.166 --> 00:05:03.235
The costumes by Lou Eyrich
are astonishing.

00:05:03.302 --> 00:05:06.205
There was no expense spared,
apparently, on this.

00:05:06.272 --> 00:05:09.074
And it was --
it was an education.

00:05:09.141 --> 00:05:10.876
And I was learning
about him, as well,

00:05:10.943 --> 00:05:12.044
and the way people
felt about him

00:05:12.110 --> 00:05:13.178
and the whole generation
of people

00:05:13.245 --> 00:05:15.682
who grew up watching him
on television

00:05:15.748 --> 00:05:18.784
being extraordinary
and being extreme on TV

00:05:18.851 --> 00:05:20.319
and coming onto shows
like this

00:05:20.386 --> 00:05:23.622
but, you know, having been up
for two days and being drunk

00:05:23.689 --> 00:05:26.391
and being wild and being bitchy.

00:05:26.458 --> 00:05:30.663
And so obviously I could watch
all of that to research it

00:05:30.730 --> 00:05:33.299
and Philip Seymour Hoffman,

00:05:33.365 --> 00:05:35.234
the great
Philip Seymour Hoffman,

00:05:35.301 --> 00:05:38.537
who, you know,
unfortunately, got an Oscar

00:05:38.604 --> 00:05:41.741
for playing the role,
so that was intimidating.

00:05:41.807 --> 00:05:44.477
But, I mean,
not that he wasn't brilliant,

00:05:44.544 --> 00:05:47.346
obviously, but, you know,
"Oh, he got an Oscar."

00:05:47.413 --> 00:05:49.748
[ Laughter ]

00:05:49.815 --> 00:05:52.919
But luckily this is
a very different story from --

00:05:52.985 --> 00:05:54.620
It's a different Truman Capote.

00:05:54.687 --> 00:05:57.590
-I'm surprised to hear
the first time

00:05:57.657 --> 00:05:59.859
you have played an American.

00:05:59.926 --> 00:06:01.627
-It is. Yeah.
-'Cause most British people,

00:06:01.694 --> 00:06:03.061
I hope you know,
play them all the time.

00:06:03.128 --> 00:06:04.363
-They only play Americans.

00:06:04.430 --> 00:06:06.098
-They're constantly
taking our jobs.

00:06:06.165 --> 00:06:07.533
I don't want to get
on my soapbox.

00:06:07.599 --> 00:06:09.769
-I know, but they do it
for less.

00:06:09.835 --> 00:06:12.771
That's why.

00:06:12.838 --> 00:06:14.440
It's just a basic --

00:06:14.506 --> 00:06:18.211
They're just
undercutting everyone, yeah.

00:06:18.277 --> 00:06:20.880
No, I always wanted
to play an American, yeah.

00:06:20.946 --> 00:06:22.882
I wanted -- been desperate
to play an American.

00:06:22.948 --> 00:06:27.319
And, yeah, here I was doing it,
and I loved it.

00:06:27.386 --> 00:06:28.855
I grew up watching
the war movies

00:06:28.921 --> 00:06:31.623
where the Americans
had much better uniforms.

00:06:31.690 --> 00:06:32.892
They just had cooler hats.

00:06:32.958 --> 00:06:35.861
They had cooler cars.

00:06:35.928 --> 00:06:37.096
They were just --

00:06:37.162 --> 00:06:39.598
And I still think Americans
are quite cool.

00:06:39.665 --> 00:06:41.200
So I --
-Give us time.

00:06:41.266 --> 00:06:42.735
-I know that's wrong.
I know.

00:06:42.802 --> 00:06:46.672
I know I'm a fool, but I do.

00:06:46.738 --> 00:06:49.609
America's a wonderful,
wonderful country.

00:06:49.675 --> 00:06:54.780
So I liked pretending
to be part of it for a minute.

00:06:54.847 --> 00:06:57.116
-Yeah, it is funny
when your first American role

00:06:57.183 --> 00:06:58.484
is Truman Capote,

00:06:58.551 --> 00:07:01.286
because I do feel like
he's weirdly like --

00:07:01.353 --> 00:07:03.188
Even though he only
could be American,

00:07:03.255 --> 00:07:06.492
it's not like he is
quintessentially American.

00:07:06.559 --> 00:07:10.129
-No, I suppose not.
Perhaps that's -- Perhaps --

00:07:10.196 --> 00:07:11.530
Would you like
to expand on that?

00:07:11.597 --> 00:07:13.332
-I guess, like --

00:07:13.399 --> 00:07:15.534
Like, what I would
like you to --

00:07:15.601 --> 00:07:17.369
I would also like to see you as,

00:07:17.436 --> 00:07:21.006
like, a flat Midwestern farmer
in something.

00:07:21.073 --> 00:07:22.541
-Yes, yes.
-You know what I mean?

00:07:22.608 --> 00:07:24.009
That's the American I want
to see Tom Hollander play.

00:07:24.076 --> 00:07:25.377
-Yeah. Or -- Yeah.

00:07:25.444 --> 00:07:28.114
Or a kind of docker somewhere
or a taxi driver.

00:07:28.180 --> 00:07:31.450
-Yeah. Do you want to do
taxi driver real quick?

00:07:31.516 --> 00:07:32.885
-Robert De Niro
in "Taxi Driver"?

00:07:32.951 --> 00:07:34.620
-No, just like any --
just do your best American --

00:07:34.687 --> 00:07:36.789
-[ American accent ] No, this
guy. I'd like to play this guy.

00:07:36.855 --> 00:07:38.524
-Yeah, we all want that.
-I want to do this.

00:07:38.591 --> 00:07:40.759
-We all want that.
-I want to do that.

00:07:40.826 --> 00:07:43.029
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:45.230 --> 00:07:48.300
-You were so wonderful
in "White Lotus,"

00:07:48.367 --> 00:07:49.735
second season of "White Lotus."

00:07:49.801 --> 00:07:51.437
I'm sure you hear it
al the time, deservedly so.

00:07:51.504 --> 00:07:53.205
-[ Normal voice] Thank you.
-And the line,

00:07:53.272 --> 00:07:55.807
"These gays are trying
to murder me," even --

00:07:55.874 --> 00:07:57.209
Jennifer Coolidge even said it

00:07:57.276 --> 00:07:58.777
in her acceptance speech
at the Emmys.

00:07:58.844 --> 00:08:01.347
I feel like it's going to be
part of -- I don't know.

00:08:01.413 --> 00:08:03.081
It's going to be a quote
for all time.

00:08:03.148 --> 00:08:04.517
-Yes.
-Do people ever yell it at you?

00:08:04.584 --> 00:08:05.951
-Not at me, no, but, I mean,

00:08:06.018 --> 00:08:07.520
they obviously
yell it at her a lot,

00:08:07.586 --> 00:08:10.622
but I'm -- they --

00:08:10.689 --> 00:08:12.157
No, they're too scared.

00:08:12.224 --> 00:08:14.627
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:08:14.693 --> 00:08:17.596
-There's a couple of lines

00:08:17.663 --> 00:08:22.101
that people come up with,
that people quote.

00:08:22.167 --> 00:08:25.771
I had a line
about potatoes in.

00:08:25.838 --> 00:08:29.007
Something about "these excellent
boiled potatoes" in one thing.

00:08:29.074 --> 00:08:32.178
-[ Cheers ]
-Yeah. See, someone --

00:08:32.244 --> 00:08:35.147
I don't even remember saying it.

00:08:35.214 --> 00:08:38.050
And a line about "difficult
difficult lemon difficult."

00:08:38.117 --> 00:08:40.285
Does anyone remember
that one from "In the Loop"?

00:08:40.352 --> 00:08:41.754
That was it. Yeah.

00:08:41.821 --> 00:08:44.256
-I think "In the Loop was maybe
the first time I saw you.

00:08:44.323 --> 00:08:45.491
That was a fantastic film.

00:08:45.557 --> 00:08:48.560
-That was very well written.
Yeah, yeah.

00:08:48.627 --> 00:08:50.262
-I have to ask you.

00:08:50.329 --> 00:08:51.963
I feel like it's almost
the elephant in the room.

00:08:52.030 --> 00:08:53.833
There's an actor
named Tom Holland.

00:08:53.899 --> 00:08:55.367
-Oh, yeah.

00:08:55.434 --> 00:08:58.803
-Does that ever -- Do people
ever make that error?

00:08:58.870 --> 00:09:01.573
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:09:01.640 --> 00:09:03.976
Yes. It's been very difficult.

00:09:04.043 --> 00:09:05.978
[ Laughter ]

00:09:06.045 --> 00:09:08.347
'Cause, you know,
I was here first.

00:09:08.414 --> 00:09:10.583
-Yeah.

00:09:10.649 --> 00:09:14.853
-But he's enormously famous,

00:09:14.920 --> 00:09:17.122
so I --

00:09:17.189 --> 00:09:19.692
Obviously, I don't actually
get mistaken for him,

00:09:19.759 --> 00:09:25.630
but in non-visual contexts, I am
mistaken for him all the time.

00:09:25.697 --> 00:09:28.567
So, like, talking to utility
companies, they just,

00:09:28.633 --> 00:09:30.302
"And what's your name?"
And they go, "Tom Holland?"

00:09:30.369 --> 00:09:32.237
'Cause they've heard
Tom Holland, Tom Holland.

00:09:32.304 --> 00:09:35.440
You go,
"No, it's Tom Hollan-der."

00:09:35.507 --> 00:09:39.078
Or I'm introduced to somebody
very, very excited,

00:09:39.144 --> 00:09:42.381
then confused,
then disappointed children

00:09:42.448 --> 00:09:47.519
who they go, "My children
are so excited to meet you."

00:09:47.586 --> 00:09:49.955
And I go, "Are they, though?"

00:09:50.021 --> 00:09:53.692
And then, "Okay, well..."
"Oh, no, they really are."

00:09:53.759 --> 00:09:57.596
"Okay, I don't think they are,
but bring them out."

00:09:57.663 --> 00:09:59.165
And they come out and they go,
"Where is he? Where is he?"

00:09:59.231 --> 00:10:01.800
And they go, "No, no."

00:10:01.867 --> 00:10:03.769
[ Laughter ]

00:10:05.804 --> 00:10:11.076
And people in the accounts
department of my agency

00:10:11.143 --> 00:10:12.377
got confused,

00:10:12.444 --> 00:10:17.182
and we were with
the same agency briefly.

00:10:17.249 --> 00:10:18.684
[ Laughter ]
And, uh...

00:10:21.520 --> 00:10:23.555
And it was a terrible moment.

00:10:23.622 --> 00:10:28.627
I went to see my friend who was
doing theater in England and --

00:10:28.694 --> 00:10:32.631
for £300 a week, you know,
but doing great work, Chekhov,

00:10:32.697 --> 00:10:35.000
and I sat smugly
in the audience,

00:10:35.067 --> 00:10:38.737
having just done a BBC show
for 30 grand or something,

00:10:38.804 --> 00:10:39.905
which was, you know,
going to get me

00:10:39.972 --> 00:10:41.440
through the next year or so.

00:10:41.506 --> 00:10:43.308
And I was thinking,
"Well, this is marvelous.

00:10:43.375 --> 00:10:46.411
I'm very prosperous,
but my good friend Peter

00:10:46.478 --> 00:10:49.414
is doing this great play,
and I'm going to go afterwards

00:10:49.481 --> 00:10:51.483
and see him
and slightly patronize him

00:10:51.550 --> 00:10:53.451
and say how wonderful it was."

00:10:53.518 --> 00:10:55.454
And then the interval came

00:10:55.520 --> 00:10:56.889
and I thought
I'd check my e-mails,

00:10:56.955 --> 00:11:00.226
and I got an e-mail
from the agency saying,

00:11:00.292 --> 00:11:03.628
"Payment advice,
your first box-office bonus

00:11:03.695 --> 00:11:05.964
for 'The Avengers.'"

00:11:06.031 --> 00:11:07.800
And, um...
[ Laughter ]

00:11:07.867 --> 00:11:09.068
And I thought...

00:11:09.135 --> 00:11:11.270
[ Laughter ]

00:11:14.339 --> 00:11:17.376
..."I don't think
I'm in 'The Avengers.'"

00:11:17.443 --> 00:11:20.345
And it was an astonishing
amount of money.

00:11:20.412 --> 00:11:21.914
And it was not his salary.

00:11:21.980 --> 00:11:25.785
It was his first
box-office bonus,

00:11:25.851 --> 00:11:28.353
not the whole box office,
the first one.

00:11:28.420 --> 00:11:30.555
And it was more money
than I've ever --

00:11:30.622 --> 00:11:32.724
It was a seven-figure sum.

00:11:32.791 --> 00:11:35.327
-Yeah, they had to send it
in two e-mails.

00:11:35.394 --> 00:11:38.497
-He was 20 or something.

00:11:38.563 --> 00:11:41.366
So my feeling of smugness
that you remember I had

00:11:41.433 --> 00:11:45.637
in the first half
disappeared very quickly.

00:11:45.704 --> 00:11:48.007
But that's showbiz.

00:11:48.073 --> 00:11:51.643
It's up, it's down,
it's hero, it's zero.

00:11:51.710 --> 00:11:53.178
-You're always one e-mail away

00:11:53.245 --> 00:11:55.581
from having your whole life
turned upside down.

00:11:55.648 --> 00:11:58.217
-Yeah.
-Congratulations on the show.

00:11:58.283 --> 00:11:59.218
Thank you so much
